From: buntOS contributors
Subject: [PATCH] drm/mediatek: do not ask for a VT switch on system sleep

Without a driver that says otherwise the kernel moves to the suspend
console around a system sleep. A compositor then loses the session as soon
as the system wakes up, drops its input devices and DRM master, and takes
them back half a second later. On the TB375FC this shows as a flicker, and
the seat losing touch is the moment a lock screen crashes.

The driver restores the display itself through the atomic helpers, like
i915 and others that opt out, so nothing needs the switch.
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/mediatek/mtk_drm_drv.c | 5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/mediatek/mtk_drm_drv.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/mediatek/mtk_drm_drv.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/mediatek/mtk_drm_drv.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/mediatek/mtk_drm_drv.c
@@ -10,6 +10,7 @@
 #include <linux/of.h>
 #include <linux/of_platform.h>
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
+#include <linux/pm.h>
 #include <linux/pm_runtime.h>
 #include <linux/dma-mapping.h>
 
@@ -781,6 +782,9 @@ static int mtk_drm_bind(struct device *dev)
 
 	drm_client_setup(drm, NULL);
 
+	/* The display is restored without the VT switch of the system sleep */
+	pm_vt_switch_required(dev, false);
+
 	return 0;
 
 err_deinit:
@@ -802,6 +806,7 @@ static void mtk_drm_unbind(struct device *dev)
 
 	/* for multi mmsys dev, unregister drm dev in mmsys master */
 	if (private->drm_master) {
+		pm_vt_switch_unregister(dev);
 		drm_dev_unregister(private->drm);
 		mtk_drm_kms_deinit(private->drm);
 		drm_dev_put(private->drm);
